    faqs: [
      { q: 'Should an iOS developer cover letter mention SwiftUI even if most of their experience is in UIKit?', a: 'Yes, but be precise about the split — write something like "SwiftUI for new feature development, UIKit for legacy maintenance" rather than overstating SwiftUI depth you don\'t have. Most Indian product companies in 2026 are building new screens in SwiftUI while maintaining older UIKit codebases, so hiring managers expect and respect an honest breakdown rather than a claim of exclusive SwiftUI expertise.' },
      { q: 'How important are App Store metrics in an iOS developer cover letter?', a: 'Very important — App Store rating, download count, and crash-free session rate are the fastest way to communicate app quality and scale to a hiring manager, similar to how GitHub stars or production metrics work for backend engineers. If your current app has a modest rating or download count, focus instead on a specific technical win — a performance optimisation with a clear before/after number — which is just as credible a signal at the individual contributor level.' },
    ],
